## 3.  Key points for production use

| Concern | How the demo deals with it | What a production system must add |
|---------|----------------------------|-----------------------------------|
| **Persistence** | In‑memory stores – data is lost on restart. | Replace `raft.NewInmemStore` with `raft.NewBoltStore` (or another durable LogStore) and a persistent SnapshotStore. |
| **Snapshotting** | Simple in‑memory snapshot (just returns all messages). | Implement incremental snapshots, periodic compaction, and use `SnapshotStore.Persist`. |
| **Membership changes** | Manual `AddPeer` calls. | Use the Raft configuration‑change API (`AddPeer`/`RemovePeer`) in a safe, coordinated way (e.g. via a membership service). |
| **Request forwarding** | When a non‑leader node receives a `Broadcast`, it retries locally forever. | Implement a tiny client‑side “redirect” – catch `ErrNotLeader`, read `raft.Leader()`, and forward the request to the current leader. |
| **Timeouts & back‑offs** | Fixed 500 ms sleep on apply error. | Exponential back‑off, jitter, and proper context cancellation. |
| **Testing** | Simple manual kill‑leader test. | Automated chaos testing (e.g., Docker‑kill, network partition tools). |
| **Monitoring** | Log messages only. | Export Prometheus/OpMetrics metrics for commit latency, leadership changes, log length, etc. |

The code above is intentionally minimal so you can see the core idea: **write every broadcast value into the Raft log and deliver it when the log entry is committed**. The Raft consensus algorithm guarantees that all correct nodes see the same log entries in the same order, which is exactly the definition of total‑order broadcast.

Feel free to extend the demo (add persistence, snapshotting, request forwarding, etc.) and adapt it to your own project. Happy coding!

